    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d424">

                <pb facs="0614" n="524" sameAs="frus:frus1934v03/pg524"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">611.9331/173: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Johnson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peiping</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1934-01-11T14:00:00+08:00">January 11, 1934—2
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received January 11—6:50 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>28. Department’s 6, January 9, 5 p.m. Note to Acting Minister for Foreign Affairs
                    bearing date January 13 has been mailed to Peck<anchor n="58" xml:id="d424-f1"/>
                    today for delivery on the 13th. The second sentence of first paragraph was
                    drafted to read as follows: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“Under date of January 3, 1934, I informed Your Excellency that your note
                            under reference had been communicated to my Government.”</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Johnson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d424-f1" n="58" xml:id="d424-f1-content">
                <p>Willys R. Peck, Counselor of Legation and Consul General at Nanking.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
